scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1859:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_43_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_43_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,"The word 'owner' in Section 40 of the Forest Ordinance should be given a purposive interpretation, considering the intention of the legislature.",In my view the word “owner” as it occurs in Section 40 of the Forest Ordinance cannot be considered in isolation applying purely legal definition of the term “owner” but must be given a purposive interpretation taking into account the intention of the legislature.,SRC001-s40,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2097:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_29_2011.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_29_2011.pdf,,SC,2011,An unwritten promise or agreement between parties is enforceable if the action is commenced within three years from the time the cause of action arose.,"I hold according to the evidence placed by documents before court that there was an unwritten promise/bargain between the parties with regard to payments for the advertisements and programs telecast after 2004 December. The cause of action to recover the dues had arisen in June, 2005 and action had been filed in September, 2006 which is within 3 years from the date of the cause of action.",SRC071-s7,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2124:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_7_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_7_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,An action to recover a debt based on an account stated is not prescribed within three years.,An action to recover the balance was prescribed in three years.,SRC071-s7,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2132:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_fr_13_2020.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_fr_13_2020.pdf,,SC,2020,"A person who has agreed to purchase a house on an installment basis can be considered as having a main document in proof of residence, and is entitled to obtain full marks for that document.","When considering the totality of the evidence placed before this Court, I hold that the 1st Petitioner is entitled to obtain full marks to the document produce marked as 9A, the main document in proof of the Residence of the Petitioner.",SRC001-s4,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2151:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_fr_311_2016-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_fr_311_2016-1.pdf,,SC,2016,A public officer cannot be transferred due to service exigencies at the whims and fancies of a superior.,"Moreover, a public officer cannot be transferred due to service exigencies at the whims and fancies of a superior.",SRC029-s114(f),none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2188:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_rule_3_2011.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_rule_3_2011.pdf,,SC,2011,"A practicing attorney-at-law and notary public who is convicted of serious offenses, including forgery and fraud, and receives a light sentence, may be suspended from practicing their profession for a period of time.","It is unimaginable that the original records and files maintained by the High Court of Colombo and the Attorney General’s Department, which are the primary institutions involved in law enforcement, could be made to disappear so easily.",SRC014-s19(2),bad-source,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:771:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKSC-1979-5-1,commonlii-LKSC-1979-5,(1995) 1 Sri LR 7,LKSC,1979,A person who holds themselves out as an agent of another may be presumed to be a sufficient medium for receiving notice on behalf of the principal.,"(1) The 1st defendant had held himself out as the agent of the other defendants and the other defendants by their own conduct held out that the 1st defendant was their sole spokesman in relation to the transaction so much as to induce Gnanam, Managing Director of the plaintiff company and the lawyers for the company that the 1st defendant was a sufficient medium through which notice could reach the other defendants. The 1st defendant did not give evidence and the court is entitled to draw the presumption that had he given evidence; such evidence would have been unfavourable to the case of the defendants - see section 114 illustration (f) of the Evidence Ordinance. (2) The draft deed of transfer was received by 1st defendant four days prior to the date fixed for signing the transfer. There was no uncertainty in relation to the vendors and vendee, the subject matter of the sale and the consideration. There was sufficient compliance on the part of the plaintiff in regard to the stipulation, of notice of the, draft. Although a plaintiff who breaks-an-essential term of the agreement cannot claim specific performance, trivial breaches do not disentitle a party from claiming relief. (3) Time was of the essence of the contract only in respect of the last date for signing the deed of transfer and in no other respect On the last date for signing, the defendants were not available and wanted a further date to sign the deed of transfer. 8 (4) The plaintiff was entitled to a decree for specific performance",SRC029-s114,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:772:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKSC-1979-5-2,commonlii-LKSC-1979-5,(1995) 1 Sri LR 7,LKSC,1979,A party who breaks an essential term of an agreement cannot claim specific performance.,"(1) The 1st defendant had held himself out as the agent of the other defendants and the other defendants by their own conduct held out that the 1st defendant was their sole spokesman in relation to the transaction so much as to induce Gnanam, Managing Director of the plaintiff company and the lawyers for the company that the 1st defendant was a sufficient medium through which notice could reach the other defendants. The 1st defendant did not give evidence and the court is entitled to draw the presumption that had he given evidence; such evidence would have been unfavourable to the case of the defendants - see section 114 illustration (f) of the Evidence Ordinance. (2) The draft deed of transfer was received by 1st defendant four days prior to the date fixed for signing the transfer. There was no uncertainty in relation to the vendors and vendee, the subject matter of the sale and the consideration. There was sufficient compliance on the part of the plaintiff in regard to the stipulation, of notice of the, draft. Although a plaintiff who breaks-an-essential term of the agreement cannot claim specific performance, trivial breaches do not disentitle a party from claiming relief. (3) Time was of the essence of the contract only in respect of the last date for signing the deed of transfer and in no other respect On the last date for signing, the defendants were not available and wanted a further date to sign the deed of transfer. 8 (4) The plaintiff was entitled to a decree for specific performance",SRC029-s114,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:773:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKSC-1979-5-3,commonlii-LKSC-1979-5,(1995) 1 Sri LR 7,LKSC,1979,Time is of the essence of a contract only in respect of specific dates agreed upon by the parties.,"(1) The 1st defendant had held himself out as the agent of the other defendants and the other defendants by their own conduct held out that the 1st defendant was their sole spokesman in relation to the transaction so much as to induce Gnanam, Managing Director of the plaintiff company and the lawyers for the company that the 1st defendant was a sufficient medium through which notice could reach the other defendants. The 1st defendant did not give evidence and the court is entitled to draw the presumption that had he given evidence; such evidence would have been unfavourable to the case of the defendants - see section 114 illustration (f) of the Evidence Ordinance. (2) The draft deed of transfer was received by 1st defendant four days prior to the date fixed for signing the transfer. There was no uncertainty in relation to the vendors and vendee, the subject matter of the sale and the consideration. There was sufficient compliance on the part of the plaintiff in regard to the stipulation, of notice of the, draft. Although a plaintiff who breaks-an-essential term of the agreement cannot claim specific performance, trivial breaches do not disentitle a party from claiming relief. (3) Time was of the essence of the contract only in respect of the last date for signing the deed of transfer and in no other respect On the last date for signing, the defendants were not available and wanted a further date to sign the deed of transfer. 8 (4) The plaintiff was entitled to a decree for specific performance",SRC029-s114,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:984:draftly-rule-courts-coa-13.-ca-phc-209_17-judgment.pdf-1,courts-coa-13.-ca-phc-209_17-judgment.pdf,,COA,,"A Magistrate cannot assume jurisdiction to hear and determine a case under Section 66 of the Primary Courts' Procedure Act if a civil action is pending in a District Court, unless there is a breach of peace.","If the mere institution of a suit in a civil Court is sufficient to divest the Magistrate of his jurisdiction, the whole purpose of Section 62 will be defeated. A scheming party will be enabled to play hide and seek",SRC001-s44,section-not-found,Section 62 of the Administration of Justice Law,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1140:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_134_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_134_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,A party alleging that a deed is a forgery must prove the execution of the deed to establish its genuineness.,"If these deeds are excluded, the Plaintiff cannot establish his title and his action will necessarily fail.",SRC029-s114,section-not-found,section 114 of the Evidence Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1172:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_15_2008.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_15_2008.pdf,,SC,2008,"A statutory presumption in favour of the execution of a judicial or official act can be made if it is likely to have happened, considering the common course of natural events, human conduct, and public and private business.","This Court rules on a statutory presumption in favour of the execution, in terms of Section 114 (d) of the Evidence Ordinance. This Section reads as follows; 4 “The Court may presume the existence of any fact which it thinks likely to have happened, regard being had to the common course of natural events, human conduct, and public and private business in their relation to the facts of the particular case – that judicial and official acts have been regularly performed.”",SRC029-s114(d),none,Section 114 (d) of the Evidence Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1253:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_32_2021.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_32_2021.pdf,,SC,2021,A party cannot take up new positions which are questions of fact for the first time on appeal.,The defendants cannot take up new positions which are questions of fact for the first time on appeal.,SRC029-s114,none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1392:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_23_2008.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_23_2008.pdf,,SC,2008,The prescription period for an action without a written agreement is three years from the date of the cause of action.,"In the case of an unwritten agreement, an action should be filed within three years from the breach of the said agreement (from the date of the cause of action), in terms of section 7 of the said Ordinance.",SRC071-s7,ok-unchecked,section 7 of the Prescription Ordinance No. 22 of 1871 as amended,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1405:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_7_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_7_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,"An action to recover a debt based on an account stated is not prescribed in three years, but in one year if it is based on goods sold and delivered.",An action to recover the balance was prescribed in three years.,SRC071-s7,ok-unchecked,Section 7 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1406:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_fr_13_2020.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_fr_13_2020.pdf,,SC,2020,A person who has agreed to purchase a house on an installment basis can use the agreement as proof of residence.,"When considering the totality of the evidence placed before this Court, I hold that the 1st Petitioner is entitled to obtain full marks to the document produce marked as 9A, the main document in proof of the Residence of the Petitioner.",SRC001-s4,none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1410:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_fr_311_2016-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_fr_311_2016-1.pdf,,SC,2016,A public officer's transfer must be based on a valid reason and the officer must be made aware of the reason.,"The PSC Rules recognise three different categories of exigencies of service, so merely stating that it was on exigencies of service is not sufficient.",SRC029-s114(f),none,Article 12 (1) of the Constitution,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1413:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_rule_3_2011.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_rule_3_2011.pdf,,SC,2011,"A practicing attorney-at-law and notary public who has been convicted of serious offenses, including forgery and fraud, and has received a light sentence, may be suspended from practicing his profession for a period of eight years.","It is unimaginable that the original records and files maintained by the High Court of Colombo and the Attorney General’s Department, which are the primary institutions involved in law enforcement, could be made to disappear so easily.",SRC014-s19(2),bad-source,"Section 42(2) of the Judicature Act No. 2 of 1978, Section 30(12) and Section 39 of the Notaries Ordinance No. 1 of 1907, as subsequently amended, Rule 60 and 61 of the Supreme Court (Conduct of and Etiquette for Attorney-at-Law) Rules 1988",False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
