scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1667:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_169_2015.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_169_2015.pdf,,SC,2015,A party may lead evidence of an informal promise to reconvey to establish a trust governed by Section 5(3) or Chapter IX of the Trust Ordinance.,"If not, as has been previously observed by this Court, said provisions of the Trust Ordinance effectively becomes nugatory.",SRC001-s2,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1670:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_170_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_170_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,"A donee's failure to provide financial assistance to a donor, especially when the donor is in need and has no other means of support, can be considered as 'other equally grave causes' of gross ingratitude.","The donee Defendant has failed to do so. She could not have acted any worse than turning a blind eye to someone who did not ask for any assistance monthly from 1992 to 2005, when asked for financial assistance of a small amount compared to the earning capacity of someone living in U.K. There are numerous ways she could have helped if she really cared to do so but she never did.",SRC001-s2,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1671:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_171_2011.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_171_2011.pdf,,SC,2011,"In a possessory action, the plaintiff need not prove lawful title to the subject matter of the action.",It is absolutely clear that a possessory action can be instituted without proof of title. The Plaintiff in a possessory action need not prove at all that he has lawful title to the subject matter of the action.,SRC071-s4,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1681:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_175_2010.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_175_2010.pdf,,SC,2010,Section 2 of the Prevention of Frauds Ordinance and Section 92 of the Evidence Ordinance do not bar parole evidence to prove a constructive trust and that the transferor did not intend to pass the beneficial interest in the property.,"I am in respectful agreement with a view expressed by His Lordship Justice Dheeraratne. After considering the above legal literature, I would like to follow the principle laid down in the case of Dayawathi and Others Vs. Gunasekera and Another (Supra) and I hold that Section 2 of the Prevention of Frauds Ordinance and Section 92 of the Evidence Ordinance do not operate as a bar to lead parole evidence to prove a constructive trust and to prove that the transferor did not intend to dispose of beneficial interest in the property.",SRC001-s2,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1682:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_175_2018.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_175_2018.pdf,,SC,2018,A document executed in terms of Section 2 of the Prevention of Frauds Ordinance is valid.,"The Document marked P2 which is the Agreement to Sell entered before Lakshmi Surige, AAL and Notary Public is a document executed in terms of Section 2 of the Prevention of Frauds Ordinance.",SRC001-s2,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1684:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_175_2025.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_175_2025.pdf,,SC,2025,A deed executed before a notary can be proved by the notary if they knew the executant personally and can testify that the signature on the deed is that of the executant.,"Where a deed executed before a notary is sought to be proved, the notary can be regarded as an attesting witness within the meaning of section 68 of the Evidence Ordinance if the notary knew the executant personally and can testify that the signature on the deed is that of the executant.",SRC029-s68,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1686:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_178_2011.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_178_2011.pdf,,SC,2011,"A party cannot adduce oral evidence against the contents of a notarially executed deed to prove fraud and/or duress/intimidation, unless the fact may be proved which will invalidate any document on the basis of fraud, intimidation, illegality, want of consideration etc.","Under section 92 of the Evidence Ordinance oral evidence could not be led which is inconsistent with the contents of the deed. Under proviso (1) to section 92 of the Evidence Ordinance any fact may be proved which will invalidate any document on the basis of fraud, intimidation, illegality, want of consideration etc.",SRC029-s92,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1693:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_181_182_2015.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_181_182_2015.pdf,,SC,2015,A person's possession of land can be considered adverse to another's interest if it continues for a long time without interruption.,"The cumulative effect of the evidence of the Plaintiff supported by the evidence given by Grama Niladhari Gama Ethige Don Francis Lambert is a clear establishment before Court that the nature of the possession of the relevant land maintained and enjoyed by the 2nd, 3rd and the 4th Defendants, was a possession which was adverse to the interest of both the Plaintiff and Gnana Jayasinghe.",SRC071-s3,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1719:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_194_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_194_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,"To claim prescriptive title under Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance, the claimant’s possession to the property should be an adverse possession or independent possession.","To claim prescriptive title under Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance, the claimant’s possession to the property should be an adverse possession or independent possession.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1724:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_197_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_197_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,A party claiming a declaration of title must have title themselves.,"There is abundant authority that, a party claiming a declaration of title must have title himself. ... The authorities unite in holding that plaintiff must show title to the corpus in dispute and that if he cannot, the action will not lie.",SRC071-s3,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1727:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_198_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_198_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,The probative value of a death certificate is limited to the details of the birth or death or still-birth to which it refers.,The probative value of the contents of a death certificate issued under both Ordinance No. 1 of 1895 and Births and Deaths Registration Act No. 17 of 1951 is limited in terms of those two enactments to the details of the birth or death or still-birth to which it refers and applies to.,SRC029-s21,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
