scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1911:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_53_2011.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_53_2011.pdf,,SC,2011,"A co-owner cannot acquire prescriptive title to undivided land, and a partition among co-owners terminates co-ownership only after undisturbed, uninterrupted, and exclusive possession of the divided lots for a period of over ten years.","Where a land is divided with the consent of all the co-owners but no cross conveyances are executed in respect of the lots, co-ownership terminates only after undisturbed, uninterrupted and exclusive possession of the divided lots for a period of over ten years",SRC071-s3,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1914:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_53_2019.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_53_2019.pdf,,SC,2019,A party invoking the provisions of section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance must establish a starting point for their acquisition of prescriptive rights with consistent and credible evidence.,"In order to defeat the ownership of an adverse claimant to immovable property, the burden of proof rests squarely and fairly on him to establish a starting point for his or her acquisition of prescriptive rights (Chelliah vs. Wijenathan 54 NLR 337, Solomon Dias vs. William Singho and others [2015] 1 SLR 277).",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1920:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_54_2020.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_54_2020.pdf,,SC,2020,A defence of prescription must be specially pleaded by way of defence and cannot be raised for the first time in appeal.,"Basnayaka CJ, in Brampy Appuhamy v Gunasekera (supra) has held that (at p. 255); “[I]t is settled law that when, as in the case of Sections 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10 and 11 of the Prescription Ordinance, the effect of the statute is merely to limit the time in which an action may be brought and not to extinguish the right, the Court will not take the statute into account unless it is specially pleaded by way of defence”.",SRC071-s5,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1922:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_54a_2008.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_54a_2008.pdf,,SC,2008,"A plaintiff must adduce adequate and reliable evidence to establish prescriptive title, including assertive evidence of adverse possession as against mere evidence of occupation.","However, the evidence of the defendant and her son does not suggest that they did any such thing. In this connection, the defendant does not claim that she made any statement to the plaintiff‟s father that she does not accept his title. The defendant does not claim that, after the plaintiff‟s father obtained title, she made any alterations to the property or paid the rates and taxes in respect of the property in her name or obtained electricity and water connections in her name. The defendant has not led the evidence of the Grama Sevaka or her neighbours to suggest that she was considered to be holding the property in her own right after the property was transferred to the plaintiff‟s father in 1978.",SRC071-s13,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1924:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_55_2015.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_55_2015.pdf,,SC,2015,"A judge must consider all prayers in a plaint, even in an ex parte trial.","Even in an ex parte trial, the judge must act according to law and ensure that the relief claimed is due in fact and in law, and must dismiss the plaintiff's claim if he is not entitled to it. An ex parte judgment cannot be entered without a hearing and an adjudication.",SRC029-s68,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1926:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_55_2020.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_55_2020.pdf,,SC,2020,A party must prove the due execution of a document to establish its validity.,"The learned judge has noted that both the 2nd Defendant and the Plaintiff have not discharged the burden cast on them by section 68 of the Evidence Ordinance read with section 2 of the Prevention of Frauds Ordinance, which required them to prove that the two documents",SRC029-s68,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1929:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_56_2023.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_56_2023.pdf,,SC,2023,A plea of prescription requires ten years of uninterrupted and undisturbed possession.,"Proof of the undisturbed and uninterrupted possession by a defendant in any action, or by those under whom he claims, of lands or immovable property, by a title adverse to or independent of that of the claimant or plaintiff in such action (that is to say, a possession unaccompanied by payment of rent or produce, or performance of service or duty, or by any other act by the possessor, from which an acknowledgment of a right existing in another person would fairly and naturally be inferred) for ten years previous to the bringing of such action, shall entitle the defendant to a decree in his favour with costs...",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
