scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1067:draftly-rule-courts-coa-1095-99-f.pdf-1,courts-coa-1095-99-f.pdf,,COA,,The burden of proving prescriptive title falls on the defendant when the legal title is in the plaintiff.,"It is settled law that where the legal title is in the plaintiff the burden of proving prescriptive title falls on the defendant (Siyanaris Vs Jayasinghe Udeni De Silva 52 NLR 289, Juliana Hamine Vs. Don Thomas 59 NLR 546, Sirajudeen and two others Vs. Abbas 1994 (2) SLR 365).",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1072:draftly-rule-courts-coa-15.-ca-dcf-1058_2000.pdf-1,courts-coa-15.-ca-dcf-1058_2000.pdf,,COA,2000,"A conditional transfer of immovable property is not considered a mortgage for the purposes of the Debt Conciliation Ordinance, and therefore, Section 43 of the Ordinance does not apply to such transfers.",The amendment to the definition of ‘mortgage’ made by Act No. 5 of 1959 has not altered the law relating to the creation of a mortgage over immovable property and has not recognized as a mode of creating a mortgage the execution of a conditional transfer.,SRC029,ok,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1085:draftly-rule-courts-coa-628-99-dc-negombo-27.2.19-autosaved.pdf-1,courts-coa-628-99-dc-negombo-27.2.19-autosaved.pdf,,COA,,A person claiming prescriptive title must show a change in the nature of the possession and an ouster.,"It is to be noted that the above admission of the 2nd Defendant creates an absurdity to the adverse possession claimed by him and also he himself admitted that the Ladis Laus Perera is the owner of the house. In Tillekaratne Vs. Bastian [(1918) 21 NLR 12] it is an established principle of law that a person who has entered into possession of land as a licensee is presumed to continue to possess it in the same capacity. Where a licensee claims that his original possession has later become adverse, he must prove of the manifestation of his intention to possess adversely to the true owner by what is sometimes referred to as an ‘overt unequivocal act’.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1087:draftly-rule-courts-coa-BOR-0001-2027.03.2026-1.pdf-1,courts-coa-BOR-0001-2027.03.2026-1.pdf,,COA,2026,"The valuation submitted by the Appellant's expert witness must be supported by evidence, both oral and documentary.","Hence, it clearly, appears that the valuation submitted by Mr. Kaleel- Private Valuer for the original Appellant, of the land intended to be acquired by the Respondent on the date of the publication of the notice under section 7 of the Act in the Gazette (A2/R7) on 03.01.1994 had not been supported by any evidence oral or documentary by the Appellant and therefore, it is without any basis and as such it cannot, sustain both in fact and law and therefore, it should be rejected as rightly, held by LABR.",SRC029-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1088:draftly-rule-courts-coa-BOR-0001-2320.02.2026.pdf-1,courts-coa-BOR-0001-2320.02.2026.pdf,,COA,2026,"The burden of proof lies on the appellants to prove what they had asserted before it in terms of section 3 of the Evidence Ordinance to be read with sections 101,102 and 103 thereof.","rden of proof will lie on the Appellants to prove what they had asserted before it in terms of section 3 of the Evidence Ordinance to be read with sections 101,102 and 103 thereof.",SRC029-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1095:draftly-rule-courts-coa-c.a-dc-135-1999f-1.pdf-1,courts-coa-c.a-dc-135-1999f-1.pdf,,COA,1999,A party invoking the provisions of section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance to defeat the ownership of an adverse claimant to immovable property must establish a starting point for their acquisition of prescriptive rights.,"Where a party invokes the provisions of section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance in order to defeat the ownership of an adverse claimant to immovable property, the burden of proof rests squarely and fairly on him to establish a starting point for his acquisition of prescriptive rights.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1096:draftly-rule-courts-coa-c.a-dc-135-1999f.pdf-1,courts-coa-c.a-dc-135-1999f.pdf,,COA,1999,A party invoking the provisions of section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance to defeat the ownership of an adverse claimant to immovable property must establish a starting point for their acquisition of prescriptive rights.,"Where a party invokes the provisions of section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance in order to defeat the ownership of an adverse claimant to immovable property, the burden of proof rests squarely and fairly on him to establish a starting point for his acquisition of prescriptive rights.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1098:draftly-rule-courts-coa-c.a-dc-326_1997.pdf-1,courts-coa-c.a-dc-326_1997.pdf,,COA,1997,The burden of proof lies on the plaintiff to show that the execution of the deed is tainted with fraud.,"When a person is bound to prove the existence of any fact, it is said that the burden of proof lies on that person.",SRC029-s101,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1103:draftly-rule-courts-coa-c.a-dc-555-1994-1.pdf-1,courts-coa-c.a-dc-555-1994-1.pdf,,COA,1994,A right of way by necessity must be granted by the court to enable a person to access a public road by the shortest way and with the least damage.,"The underlying principle seems to be that laid down in the opinion of Grotius which is followed in De Vaas vs. Mendis (1948) 49 N.L.R. 525, namely that where a party proves that he has",SRC071-s3,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1106:draftly-rule-courts-coa-c.a-dc-785-1998.pdf-1,courts-coa-c.a-dc-785-1998.pdf,,COA,1998,"In a Rei-Vindicatio action, the Plaintiff must prove his title and cannot rely on the weakness of the Defendant's title.","It is settled law that in a Rei-Vindicatio action, the Plaintiff must prove his title in establishing his rights. The Plaintiff cannot rely on the weakness of the Defendant's title.",SRC071-s3,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1112:draftly-rule-courts-coa-c.a-dcf-858-1996.pdf-1,courts-coa-c.a-dcf-858-1996.pdf,,COA,1996,"A person who is let into occupation of property as a licensee must be deemed to continue to occupy on the same footing, and to prove possession, there must be some overt act which manifests their intention to occupy.","A person who is let into occupation of property as a licensee must be deemed to continue to occupy on the same footing. Therefore, in order to prove possession there must be some overt act which manifest his intention to occupying.",SRC071-s3,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1118:draftly-rule-courts-coa-c.a-phc_155_2014.pdf-1,courts-coa-c.a-phc_155_2014.pdf,,COA,2014,A party is not entitled in law to claim a servitude of right of way over a road reservation granted by the state.,"The learned Counsel for the Respondent has referred to the case of Aflaflda Saratll Paraflagama v. Dhammadiflna Sarath Paranagama and Others (CA (PHC) APN 11712013 where A. W.A. Salam J. held that; ""Unlike in the case of a dispute relating to possession of immovable property, no time frame has been laid down to the length of time during which the right should have been enjoyed in relation to the purported entitlement. In resolving such a dispute the Judge of the Primary Court is expected to determine as 10 who is entitled to the right which is the subject mailer of the dispute and make an Order under Section 69(2). """,SRC001-s1,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1125:draftly-rule-courts-coa-c.a.-dc_1043_1997.pdf-1,courts-coa-c.a.-dc_1043_1997.pdf,,COA,1997,"The burden of seeking and getting evidence before court, in the course of investigation of title to the land sought to be partitioned by parties before Court, prior to deciding what share should go to which party is more the duty of the judge than the contesting parties.","The burden of seeking and getting evidence before court, in the course of investigation of title to the land sought to be partitioned by parties before Court, prior to deciding what share should go to which party is more the duty of the judge than the contesting parties.",SRC029-s110,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1132:draftly-rule-courts-coa-c.a.-dc_657_2000.pdf-1,courts-coa-c.a.-dc_657_2000.pdf,,COA,2000,"A court can only investigate title in a partition action within the limits of pleadings, admissions, points of contest, evidence both documentary and oral.","Although there is a duty cast on the court to investigate title in a partition action, the court can do so only within the limits of pleadings, admissions, points of contest, evidence both documentary and oral. The court cannot go on a voyage of discovery tracing the title and finding the shares in the corpus for them [Thilagaratnam v. Athpunathan and Others (1996) 2 Sri.L.R. 66].",SRC029-s90,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1134:draftly-rule-courts-coa-c.a.-dc_769-a-b_1996.pdf-1,courts-coa-c.a.-dc_769-a-b_1996.pdf,,COA,1996,Non-execution of a writ of possession does not prevent the successors of the judgment-creditor from claiming ownership of the land.,"Therefore, I hold that non-execution of the writ of possession does not prevent the successors of G. D. Clarice from claiming the ownership of the said lands by virtue of the judgment in D. C. Panadura Case No. 12417.",SRC071-s3,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1144:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca-1171-1998-f-18.1.2019.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca-1171-1998-f-18.1.2019.pdf,,COA,2019,A person who claims prescriptive title against the rightful owner who has the paper title has a very heavy burden to prove all the requirements prescribed in the Prescription Ordinance.,A person who claims prescriptive title against the rightful owner who has the paper title has a very heavy burden to prove all the requirements prescribed in section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance.,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1149:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca-423-2000-f-22.1.2019.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca-423-2000-f-22.1.2019.pdf,,COA,2019,A person who claims prescriptive title against the rightful owner who has the paper title has a very heavy burden to prove all the requirements prescribed in section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance.,A person who claims prescriptive title against the rightful owner who has the paper title has a very heavy burden to prove all the requirements prescribed in section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance.,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1154:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca-669-670-2000-f-28.10.2019.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca-669-670-2000-f-28.10.2019.pdf,,COA,2019,The person claiming prescriptive title must prove all the ingredients stated in section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance to succeed on a claim of prescription.,The person claiming prescriptive title shall prove all the ingredients stated in section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance in order to succeed on a claim of prescription.,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1165:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca-dc-kuliyapitiya-595-94f.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca-dc-kuliyapitiya-595-94f.pdf,,COA,,A co-owner cannot claim the share of another co-owner if they have possessed the property as a co-owner accepting the ownership of another co-owner.,"Consequently, the defendant had possessed the property through out as a co-owner accepting the ownership of another co￾owner. In that event the defendant cannot claim the share of the other co￾owner.",SRC071-s3,none,fuzzy,ratio,medium,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1170:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca-dcf-447-2000-18.9.2020.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca-dcf-447-2000-18.9.2020.pdf,,COA,2020,"In a rei vindicatio action, the burden is fairly and squarely on the plaintiff to prove title to the land in the manner pleaded in the plaint.","In a rei vindicatio action such as this, the burden is fairly and squarely on the Plaintiff to prove title to the land in the manner pleaded in the plaint.",SRC001-s2,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1174:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca-dcf-859-1998.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca-dcf-859-1998.pdf,,COA,1998,A party cannot claim prescriptive rights to a land if the date of commencement of adverse possession is specified to thwart the other party's attempt to claim the land on prescriptive possession.,"Without offering an explanation for the basis upon which the Plaintiffs say the Defendant commenced adverse possession of the land, the specific date mentioned in the plaint is misleading and intended to thwart any attempt on the part of the Defendant to claim the land on prescriptive possession, which he would have been entitled to do had the date of his commencement of adverse possession been a mere eight and a half months prior to that claimed by the Plaintiffs.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1179:draftly-rule-courts-coa-CA-PHC-124-22-Judgment-Final.pdf-1,courts-coa-CA-PHC-124-22-Judgment-Final.pdf,,COA,,The lessor retains proprietary rights in the leased equipment and is entitled to recover possession upon default.,"Under the Leasing Act, a finance lease is an arrangement whereby possession and use of equipment are transferred to the lessee while ownership remains vested in the lessor.",SRC001-s15,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1180:draftly-rule-courts-coa-CA-PHC-126-20-Judgment-Final.pdf-1,courts-coa-CA-PHC-126-20-Judgment-Final.pdf,,COA,,The pendency of civil proceedings does not constitute a legal bar to the institution or continuation of proceedings under Section 66.,"At its highest, the existence of such proceedings may be considered as surrounding circumstance, however, it does not undermine the Magistrate’s duty to inquire into the existence of a dispute affecting land and the likelihood of a breach of the peace, and to make such provisional orders as are necessary in that regard.",SRC001-s2,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1187:draftly-rule-courts-coa-CA-PHC-44-18-final.pdf-1,courts-coa-CA-PHC-44-18-final.pdf,,COA,,"Revisionary intervention is available where a subordinate court proceeds upon a misconception of its statutory powers, grants relief beyond the scope of its jurisdiction, fails to identify the jurisdictional facts required by statute, or reaches a determination unsupported by the material before it.","Revision is not intended to function as a further appeal. However, where a subordinate court proceeds upon a misconception of its statutory powers, grants relief beyond the scope of its jurisdiction, fails to identify the jurisdictional facts required by statute, or reaches a determination unsupported by the material before it, revisionary intervention becomes available.",SRC001-s2,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1215:draftly-rule-courts-coa-CA-Writ-742-24-final.pdf-1,courts-coa-CA-Writ-742-24-final.pdf,,COA,,The Board shall consider all circumstances of the case when deciding whether a transfer of immovable property is in reality a mortgage.,"21A. (1) In any proceedings under this Ordinance in regard to an application relating to a transfer or conditional transfer of immovable property, the Board shall, notwithstanding anything to the contrary in section 2 of the Prevention of Frauds Ordinance (Chapter 70), or sections 91 and 92 of the Evidence Ordinance (Chapter 14) and for the purpose of deciding whether or not such transfer or conditional transfer is in reality a mortgage, take into consideration all the circumstances of the case and in particular the following matters:-",SRC001-s2,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1232:draftly-rule-courts-coa-CA.PHC_.-0046.2019-27.09.2024.pdf-1,courts-coa-CA.PHC_.-0046.2019-27.09.2024.pdf,,COA,2024,The Chairman of the LRC qualifies as a competent authority under the Act.,"Based on the analysis above, I conclude that the Chairman of the LRC qualifies as a competent authority under the Act.",SRC001-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1233:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca.phc_.65.03.final_.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca.phc_.65.03.final_.pdf,,COA,,"A Primary Court Judge can determine who is entitled to a right in or respecting the land, and make an order declaring that person's entitlement until they are deprived of it by a competent court.","An order under this subsection may declare that any person specified therein shall be entitled to any such right in or respecting the land or in any part of the land as may be specified in the order until such person is deprived of such right by virtue of an order or decree of a competent court, and prohibit all disturbance or interference with the exercise of such right by such party other than under the authority of an order or decree as aforesaid.",SRC001-s2,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1248:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_1160_00.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_1160_00.pdf,,COA,,A person who asserts the existence of a fact has the burden of proof.,lithe burden of proof in a suit or proceeding lies on that person who would fail if no evidence at all were given on either side,SRC029-s102,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1254:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_1295_1998.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_1295_1998.pdf,,COA,1998,"A tenant cultivator's rights are decided by the prevalent law, such as the Paddy Lands Act or Agricultural Lands Law, and cannot be changed without strong evidence to satisfy the requirements of Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance.","When a person enters a property in a particular capacity i. e tenant cultivator, his rights would be decided on the prevalent law namely the Paddy Lands Act or Agricultural Lands Law depending on the relevant period.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1259:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_242_99.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_242_99.pdf,,COA,,The burden of proof rests squarely and fairly on the defendant to establish a starting point for their acquisition of prescriptive rights.,"According to section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance such a possession must be undisturbed, uninterrupted, adverse to or independent of that of the former possessor and should have lasted for at least ten years before he could transform such possession into prescriptive title.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1264:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_476_1999.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_476_1999.pdf,,COA,1999,"The property which is the subject of the act of spoliation must be restored to the person from whom it was taken, irrespective of the question as to who is in law entitled to be in possession of such property.","Spoliatus ante omnia restituendus est., which means",SRC071-s4,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1268:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_513_98.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_513_98.pdf,,COA,,The burden of proof is on the defendant in an action for declaration of title to property where the legal title is in the plaintiff but the property is in the possession of the defendant.,"IN an action for declaration of title to property, where the legal title is in the Plaintiff but the property is in the possession of the Defendant, the burden of proof is on the Defendant - See. Siyaneris vs Jayasinghe Udenis de Silva 52 NLR 289(PC) .",SRC071-s3,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1270:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_529_96.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_529_96.pdf,,COA,,A party claiming prescriptive title must prove their possession and adverse possession of the land.,The defendant's position in the District Court was that even if the plaintiff and her co owners and their predecessors in title may have had paper title to lots 2A and 28 that title had been extinguished by prescription thereof by the defendants and their predecessors in title.,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1277:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_580_1999.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_580_1999.pdf,,COA,1999,A defendant claiming prescriptive title must prove the ingredients of prescription as stated in Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance.,The burden is on them to prove the ingredients of prescription as stated in Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance vide page 5 second para of the judgment.,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1285:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_764_99.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_764_99.pdf,,COA,,"A lessee or licensee will not be permitted to question the title of the person who gave them the lease or license, or set up a want of title in that person.","Whether it is a licensee or lessee, the question of title is foreign to a suit in ejectment against either, the licensee obtaining possession is deemed to obtain it upon the terms that he will not dispute the title of the Plaintiff without whose permission he would not have got it.",SRC029-s116,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1287:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_788_97.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_788_97.pdf,,COA,,The burden of proof shifts to the defendant to show independent rights in the form of prescription once the plaintiff's paper title is undisputed.,Once paper title became undisputed the burden shifted to the defendants to show that they had independent rights in the form of prescription as claimed by them,SRC029-s68,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1289:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_823_98.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_823_98.pdf,,COA,,A party's admission of a fact in a proceeding need not be proved.,Section 58 of the Evidence Ordinance states that what is admitted by the parties need not be proved.,SRC029-s58,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1298:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_955_1996.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_955_1996.pdf,,COA,1996,A contract may be avoided by the court on the ground of laesio enormis either when the purchaser pays more than double the true value of the thing or the vendor sells the thing for less than half its value.,A contract may be avoided by Court on the ground of laesio enormis either when the purchaser pays more than double the true value of the thing or the vendor sells the thing for less than half its value.,SRC029-s101,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1304:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_badulla_1162_99.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_badulla_1162_99.pdf,,COA,,A person who enters into possession of a land in one capacity is presumed to continue in possession in the same capacity.,"a person who enters into possession of a land in one capacity, is presumed to continue in possession in the same capacity and a co-owner's possession in law tantamount to the possession of his other co-owners.",SRC071-s3,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1307:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_balapitiya_830_98.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_balapitiya_830_98.pdf,,COA,,A party invoking the provisions of Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance to defeat an adverse claimant's ownership of immovable property must establish a starting point for their prescriptive rights.,"Where a party invokes the provisions of Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance in order to defeat the ownership of an adverse claimant to immovable property, the burden of proof rests squarely and fairly on him to establish a starting point for his or her acquisition of prescriptive rights.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1310:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_colombo_16_99.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_colombo_16_99.pdf,,COA,,"A defendant must establish that they have possessed the land in question for a period of 10 years immediately prior to the institution of the action, as contemplated in section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance.","proof of undisturbed an uninterrupted possession by a defendant in any action, or by those under whom he claims, of immovable property, by a title adverse to or independent of that of the claimants or plaintiff in such action that is to say a possesswn unaccompanied by payment of rent of produce or performance of service or duty or by any other act by the possessor from which an acknowledgement of the right existing in another person would fairly and naturally be inferred for 10 years previous to the bringing of the action.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1314:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_colombo_780_98.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_colombo_780_98.pdf,,COA,,"A plaintiff can claim prescriptive title to land if they have been in uninterrupted possession of the land for ten years, without paying rent or performing any service or duty.",This position in law is supported by many authorities. Maasdorp's Institutes (7th Edition Vol2 at 96) Wigneswaran J in Leisa and another V Simon and Another [2002 (1) SLR 148] Pathirana V Jayasundera [58 NLR 169] Dona Cecilia V Cecilia Perera and others [1987 (1) SLR 235] Wijesundara and others v Constantine Dasa and another [1987 (2) SLR 66] I will now turn to consider whether the learned District Judge correctly evaluated the evidence and applied the law relevant thereto when he accepted the prescriptive rights .I 3 of the plaintiff rejecting the defence taken up by the defendant.,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1316:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_galle_1186_98.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_galle_1186_98.pdf,,COA,,A plaintiff cannot claim prescriptive rights to land when they are not certain that it belongs to the defendant.,"Hence, it is clear that the appellant could not have claimed prescriptive rights to the disputed land when he was not certain that it belonged to the respondent when claiming possession adverse to his rights.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1317:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_galle_123_99.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_galle_123_99.pdf,,COA,,A party invoking the provisions of Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance to defeat ownership must establish a starting point for their acquisition of prescriptive rights.,"lt is trite law that where a party to an action for a declaration of title to a land invokes the Provisions of Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance in order to defeat the ownership of an adverse claimant to immovable property, the burden of proof rests squarely and fairly on him to establish a starting point for his or her acquisition of prescriptive rights.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1320:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_galle_1292_98.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_galle_1292_98.pdf,,COA,,A party claiming prescriptive title must satisfy the court that they have established the ingredients in Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance.,In order to prescribe to the said lots 9 and 10 it is important for the party concerned to satisfy Court that he has established the ingredients in Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance.,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1324:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_gampaha_1133_00.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_gampaha_1133_00.pdf,,COA,,"An oral agreement affecting immovable property is not enforceable in law unless it is in writing and signed by the party making the same, or by some person lawfully authorized by him or her, in the presence of a licensed notary public and two or more witnesses present at the same time.","In terms of Section 2 of the Prevention of Fraud Ordinance no contract or agreement affecting land or other immovable property shall be of force or avail in law unless the same shall be in writing and signed by the party making the same, or by some person lawfully authorized by him or her in the presence of a licensed notary public and two or more witnesses present at the same time, and unless the execution of such writing, deed, or instrument be duly attested by such notary and witnesses.",SRC001-s2,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1330:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_homagama_1113_98.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_homagama_1113_98.pdf,,COA,,"A person can claim prescriptive title to land if they have been in possession of the land for a period of over ten years, adverse to the rights of the original owner.","In the circumstances, it is abundantly clear that the respondent had been in possession of the land claimed by the plaintiff for a period of well over ten years prior to the filing of this action on 03.05.1991, adverse to the rights of the plaintiff which is the criteria applicable to claim prescriptive title in terms of Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1344:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_kegalle_842_99.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_kegalle_842_99.pdf,,COA,,A lessee who has entered into occupation is precluded from disputing his lessor's title until he has first restored the property in fulfilment of his contractual obligations.,Indeed a lessee who has entered into occupation is precluded from disputing his lessor's title until he has first restored the property in fulfilment of his contractual obligation.,SRC029-s116,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1345:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_kuliyapitiya_293_00.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_kuliyapitiya_293_00.pdf,,COA,,A claim for recovery of property belonging to a temple is not barred by prescription.,"In the case of any claim for the recovery of any property, movable or immovable, belonging or alleged to belong to any temple, or for the assertion of title to any such property, the claim shall not be held to be barred or prejudiced by any provision of the Prescription Ordinance:",SRC071-s34,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1352:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_mathugama_367_1998.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_mathugama_367_1998.pdf,,COA,1998,The burden of proof rests squarely and fairly on the defendant to establish a starting point for their acquisition of prescriptive rights.,"In a case of this nature once the Plaintiff-Respondent proves title by way of a transfer deed and seek to establish paper title, the burden is on the Defendant to establish superior title by prescriptive rights. i.e to satisfy the requirements of Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
