scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1487:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_105_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_105_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,A party who claims prescriptive title to a particular corpus must demonstrate possession over a specifically identified corpus.,"A person who claims a prescriptive title must demonstrate possession over a specifically identified corpus; failure to do so renders such a claim untenable, as a prescription cannot operate over an uncertain or unidentified land.",SRC029-s58,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1489:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_105_2015.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_105_2015.pdf,,SC,2015,A party cannot acquire prescriptive title to a portion of land unless they can establish that their predecessors in title also possessed that portion in the manner set out by the relevant law.,"However, no such acceptable evidence was led before the District Court.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1490:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_105a_2008.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_105a_2008.pdf,,SC,2008,"An absolute owner of a vehicle must show that they took all possible precautions to prevent the vehicle's use in the commission of an offence, and that the offence was committed without their knowledge.",It is therefore quite clear that it would be necessary for the owner to show that he has taken all possible precautions to prevent the use of the vehicle for the commission of the offence.,SRC001-s1,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1497:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_108_2022-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_108_2022-1.pdf,,SC,2022,A mere presence of a prayer for a declaration of title cannot make the action a rei vindicatio action.,Mere presence of a prayer for a declaration of title cannot make the action a re vidicatio action.,SRC029-s116,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1499:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_10_2013.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_10_2013.pdf,,SC,2013,Mere possession does not suffice to prove prescription; adverse possession must be established.,"If that be so it would become adverse from 06.02.2003 or 07.02.2003 by forcible erection of fence, on the western boundary to lot 21. Plaintiff instituted action on 02.06.2003 which is shortly after the above erection of the fence. Therefore the period of 10 years cannot be contemplated or computed in this background.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1506:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_111_2019.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_111_2019.pdf,,SC,2019,The burden of proof shifts to the defendant to show independent rights in the form of prescription once paper title is undisputed.,Once paper title becomes undisputed the burden shifts to the defendant to show that they had independent rights in the form of prescription as claimed by them.,SRC071-s3,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1511:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_113_2019.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_113_2019.pdf,,SC,2019,A party can tag on to the possession of their predecessors to claim prescriptive title.,"In fact, a party can. [ see Terunnanse v Menike 1 N L R 200, Wijesundara and others V Constantine Dasa and Another (1987) 2 Sri L R 66, Kirihamy Muhandirama V Dingiri Appu 6 N L R 197].",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1513:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_114_2018.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_114_2018.pdf,,SC,2018,The propounder of the will has the initial burden of establishing that the will is genuine and to remove any doubts that may arise from the conscience of the court.,"Thus, the Propounder of the Last Will has to prove that the Last Will is an act or deed of the Testatrix while having the burden to remove any suspicion attached to the Last Will.",SRC001-s2,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1520:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_117_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_117_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,"Parole evidence is permitted to prove a constructive trust between parties, despite Section 2 of the Prevention of Frauds Ordinance and Section 92 of the Evidence Ordinance.","In order to prove a constructive trust, is parole evidence permitted in view of the principles set out in Section 2 of the Prevention of Fraud Ordinance and Section 92 of the Evidence Ordinance? This question was discussed in the case of Dayawathi and Others Vs Gunasekara and Another [1991] 1 SLR 115.",SRC001-s2,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1532:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_120_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_120_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,Prescription begins to run from the date of the demand in an overdraft case.,"In the present case, prescription begins to run from the date of the demand.",SRC071-s7,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1534:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_120_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_120_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,A power of attorney granted by a minor is void if the transaction is not for the benefit of the minor.,"It appears that she had considered that the Plaintiff, as per his age, was a minor in terms of our law at the time the said Power of Attorney was given and when the transaction is not for the benefit of the minor, it is not valid against the minor, and as such the Power of Attorney and the transactions based on that Power of Attorney are void.",SRC071-s10,bad-source,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1535:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_121_2011.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_121_2011.pdf,,SC,2011,Declarations regarding relationship found in documents are relevant and admissible under sections 32(5) and 32(6) of the Evidence Ordinance.,"It is under these provisions, to wit, section 32(5) and (6) and section 50 of the Evidence Ordinance that it is possible to admit evidence, otherwise hearsay, of deceased persons figuring in a genealogical tree such as one often comes across in a partition case.",SRC029-s32,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1540:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_122_2013.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_122_2013.pdf,,SC,2013,Non-compliance with the rules in Sec. 31 of the Notaries Ordinance does not invalidate the Deed as provided for by Sec. 33 of the same Ordinance.,non compliance with the rules in Sec. 31 of the Notaries Ordinance does not invalidate the Deed as provided for by Sec. 33 of the same Ordinance; that section protects the deed.,SRC014-s31,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1555:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_126_2015.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_126_2015.pdf,,SC,2015,A claim of Laesio Enormis cannot be maintained if the seller knew the true value of the property at the time of sale.,"Citing relevant judgments pronounced in this regard by our Superior Courts, it was contended that under such circumstances, a claim of Laesio Enormis cannot be maintained.",SRC071-s10,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1562:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_128_2019.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_128_2019.pdf,,SC,2019,"If the language of the enactment is clear and unambiguous, it would not be legitimate for the Courts to add words by implication into the language.","If the language of the enactment is clear and unambiguous, it would not be legitimate for the Courts to add words by implication into the language. It is a settled law of interpretation that the words are to be interpreted as they appear in the provision, simple and grammatical meaning is to be given to them, and nothing can be added or subtracted.",SRC071-s9,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1563:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_129_2010.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_129_2010.pdf,,SC,2010,A dedication of immovable property to the Sangha or a Buddhist temple does not become invalid by the absence of a notarial document.,That a dedication once made is not rendered ineffective by the absence of a notarial document executed in accordance with the Prevention of Fraud Ordinance.,SRC001-s2,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1566:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_12_2006.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_12_2006.pdf,,SC,2006,The burden of proof lies on the party asserting that the condition of the land has changed.,"When a person is bound to prove the existence of any fact, it is said that the burden of proof lies on that person.",SRC029-s101,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1570:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_131_2011.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_131_2011.pdf,,SC,2011,Mere use of a footpath does not establish a servitude of right of way by prescription.,Merely walking on a footpath does not make it an adverse act conflicting with the rights of the owner.,SRC071-s2,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1575:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_132_2018.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_132_2018.pdf,,SC,2018,A person can acquire land through adverse possession by possessing it openly and as if they were the owner for an uninterrupted period of ten years or more.,"For a comparable analysis, let me refer to the South African position. Section 1 of the Prescription Act, No. 68 of 1969 in South Africa reads as follows: “Subject to the provisions of this Chapter and of Chapter IV, a person shall by prescription become the owner of a thing which he has possessed openly and as if he were the owner thereof for an uninterrupted period of thirty years or for a period which, together with any periods for which such thing was so possessed by his predecessors in title, constitutes an uninterrupted period of 30 years.”",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1584:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_135_2023.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_135_2023.pdf,,SC,2023,"A fiscal's conveyance is not required for title to pass in a fiscal sale, and possession by the purchaser for the prescriptive period establishes title by prescription.","If a fiscal’s conveyance is not executed in favour of the purchaser of a land at a fiscal sale, no title passes. If, however, the purchaser enters into possession after the sale and possessed the land for longer than the prescriptive period, title by prescription is established.",SRC071-s13,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1604:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_144_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_144_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,"Proof of prescriptive title against co-owners is more difficult. Unless the contrary is proved by cogent evidence, possession of one co-owner is in law the possession of the other co-owners.","Unless the contrary is proved by cogent evidence, possession of one co-owner is in law the possession of the other co-owners. (Corea v. Iseris Appuhamy (1911) 15 NLR 65, Sediris Appuhamy v. James Appuhamy (1958) 60 NLR 297 at 302-303, Rajapaksha v. Hendrick Singho (1959) 61 NLR 32, Maria Perera v. Albert Perera [1983] 2 Sri LR 399, Wickremaratne and Another v. Alpenis Perera [1986] 1 Sri LR 190 at 195, Maria Fernando v. Anthony Fernando [1997] 2 Sri LR 356)",SRC071-s3,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1621:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_151_2011.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_151_2011.pdf,,SC,2011,The burden of proof lies on the party who wishes the court to believe in the existence of a fact.,"In terms of Section 103, since the Appellant has raised issues disputing that P 1 and P 2 is forgery he is the person who wishes the court to believe the existence of that fact. Hence the burden of proof lies on the Appellant to prove the existence of a forgery.",SRC029-s103,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1623:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_151_2015.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_151_2015.pdf,,SC,2015,A co-owner's possession is in law the possession of all the other co-owners.,Every co-owner is presumed to be in possession in his capacity as a co-owner.,SRC071-s3,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1624:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_151_2023.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_151_2023.pdf,,SC,2023,"A party asserting prescriptive title must discharge the burden of proof to establish uninterrupted, undisturbed, and adverse possession.","In the absence of such proof, the burden must be held not to have been discharged.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1635:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_155_2011.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_155_2011.pdf,,SC,2011,The occupation of the premises must be of such character as is incompatible with the title of the owner.,The occupation of the premises must be of such character as is incompatible with the title of the owner.,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1636:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_155_2022.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_155_2022.pdf,,SC,2022,A plea of prescription requires merely ten years of uninterrupted and undisturbed possession.,"Proof of the undisturbed and uninterrupted possession by a defendant in any action, or by those under whom he claims, of lands or immovable property, by a title adverse to or independent of that of the claimant or plaintiff in such action (that is to say, a possession unaccompanied by payment of rent or produce, or performance of service or duty, or by any other act by the possessor, from which an acknowledgment of a right existing in another person would fairly and naturally be inferred) for ten years previous to the bringing of such action, shall entitle the defendant to a decree in his favour with costs...",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1646:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_159_2019.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_159_2019.pdf,,SC,2019,"To claim prescriptive title, a party must prove possession by a title adverse to or independent of that of the claimant or plaintiff, with a specific starting point and documentary evidence.",Where a party invokes the provisions of Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance in order to defeat the ownership of an adverse claimant to immovable property the burden of proof rests fairly and squarely on him to establish the starting point for his or her acquisition of prescriptive rights [Gratiean J in Chelliah v Wijenathan 54 N.L.R. 337 at 342].,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1647:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_15_2008.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_15_2008.pdf,,SC,2008,"A court may presume the existence of any fact which it thinks likely to have happened, regard being had to the common course of natural events, human conduct, and public and private business in their relation to the facts of the particular case – that judicial and official acts have been regularly performed.",This evidence contained in the affidavit has not been challenged either by raising an issue on this matter or calling the Fiscal Officer who executed the writ and eliciting the fact that possession had not been handed over as claimed by the Appellant. No independent evidence was led to rebut this presumption.,SRC029-s114(d),bad-source,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1651:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_160_2013.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_160_2013.pdf,,SC,2013,"Parole evidence can be used to prove a constructive trust, even if the transferor did not intend to pass the beneficial interest in the property.",The Prevention of Frauds Ordinance and Sec. 92 of the Evidence Ordinance do not bar parole evidence to prove a constructive trust and that the transferor did not intend to pass the beneficial interest in the property.,SRC001-s2,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1665:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_167_2010.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_167_2010.pdf,,SC,2010,"Parole evidence can be led to prove that there was no agreement which was intended to be so, contained in the document.","Parole evidence can be led to prove that there was no agreement which was intended to be so, contained in the document.",SRC001-s92,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1672:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_169_2015.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_169_2015.pdf,,SC,2015,A party may lead evidence of an informal promise to reconvey to establish a trust governed by Section 5(3) or Chapter IX of the Trust Ordinance.,"If not, as has been previously observed by this Court, said provisions of the Trust Ordinance effectively becomes nugatory.",SRC001-s2,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1675:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_170_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_170_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,"A donee's failure to provide financial assistance to a donor, especially when the donor is in need and has no other means of support, can be considered as 'other equally grave causes' of gross ingratitude.","The donee Defendant has failed to do so. She could not have acted any worse than turning a blind eye to someone who did not ask for any assistance monthly from 1992 to 2005, when asked for financial assistance of a small amount compared to the earning capacity of someone living in U.K. There are numerous ways she could have helped if she really cared to do so but she never did.",SRC001-s2,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1676:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_171_2011.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_171_2011.pdf,,SC,2011,"In a possessory action, the plaintiff need not prove lawful title to the subject matter of the action.",It is absolutely clear that a possessory action can be instituted without proof of title. The Plaintiff in a possessory action need not prove at all that he has lawful title to the subject matter of the action.,SRC071-s4,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1686:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_175_2010.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_175_2010.pdf,,SC,2010,Section 2 of the Prevention of Frauds Ordinance and Section 92 of the Evidence Ordinance do not bar parole evidence to prove a constructive trust and that the transferor did not intend to pass the beneficial interest in the property.,"I am in respectful agreement with a view expressed by His Lordship Justice Dheeraratne. After considering the above legal literature, I would like to follow the principle laid down in the case of Dayawathi and Others Vs. Gunasekera and Another (Supra) and I hold that Section 2 of the Prevention of Frauds Ordinance and Section 92 of the Evidence Ordinance do not operate as a bar to lead parole evidence to prove a constructive trust and to prove that the transferor did not intend to dispose of beneficial interest in the property.",SRC001-s2,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1687:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_175_2018.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_175_2018.pdf,,SC,2018,A document executed in terms of Section 2 of the Prevention of Frauds Ordinance is valid.,"The Document marked P2 which is the Agreement to Sell entered before Lakshmi Surige, AAL and Notary Public is a document executed in terms of Section 2 of the Prevention of Frauds Ordinance.",SRC001-s2,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1689:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_175_2025.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_175_2025.pdf,,SC,2025,A deed executed before a notary can be proved by the notary if they knew the executant personally and can testify that the signature on the deed is that of the executant.,"Where a deed executed before a notary is sought to be proved, the notary can be regarded as an attesting witness within the meaning of section 68 of the Evidence Ordinance if the notary knew the executant personally and can testify that the signature on the deed is that of the executant.",SRC029-s68,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1691:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_178_2011.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_178_2011.pdf,,SC,2011,"A party cannot adduce oral evidence against the contents of a notarially executed deed to prove fraud and/or duress/intimidation, unless the fact may be proved which will invalidate any document on the basis of fraud, intimidation, illegality, want of consideration etc.","Under section 92 of the Evidence Ordinance oral evidence could not be led which is inconsistent with the contents of the deed. Under proviso (1) to section 92 of the Evidence Ordinance any fact may be proved which will invalidate any document on the basis of fraud, intimidation, illegality, want of consideration etc.",SRC029-s92,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1698:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_181_182_2015.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_181_182_2015.pdf,,SC,2015,A person's possession of land can be considered adverse to another's interest if it continues for a long time without interruption.,"The cumulative effect of the evidence of the Plaintiff supported by the evidence given by Grama Niladhari Gama Ethige Don Francis Lambert is a clear establishment before Court that the nature of the possession of the relevant land maintained and enjoyed by the 2nd, 3rd and the 4th Defendants, was a possession which was adverse to the interest of both the Plaintiff and Gnana Jayasinghe.",SRC071-s3,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1724:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_194_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_194_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,"To claim prescriptive title under Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance, the claimant’s possession to the property should be an adverse possession or independent possession.","To claim prescriptive title under Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance, the claimant’s possession to the property should be an adverse possession or independent possession.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1729:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_197_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_197_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,A party claiming a declaration of title must have title themselves.,"There is abundant authority that, a party claiming a declaration of title must have title himself. ... The authorities unite in holding that plaintiff must show title to the corpus in dispute and that if he cannot, the action will not lie.",SRC071-s3,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1732:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_198_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_198_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,The probative value of a death certificate is limited to the details of the birth or death or still-birth to which it refers.,The probative value of the contents of a death certificate issued under both Ordinance No. 1 of 1895 and Births and Deaths Registration Act No. 17 of 1951 is limited in terms of those two enactments to the details of the birth or death or still-birth to which it refers and applies to.,SRC029-s21,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1741:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_1_2016.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_1_2016.pdf,,SC,2016,A document marked 'P3' without objection at the closure of the case is deemed to be admitted as evidence.,"Therefore, at this juncture, the document marked ‘P3’ ought to be admitted as evidence for all purposes of law considering the fact that no objection was taken at the closure of the Plaintiffs-Appellants case.",SRC029-s114,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1751:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_20_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_20_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,The Exchange Control Act applies to the facts of this case.,"In view of what is stated above, this Court holds that the Exchange Control Act applies to the facts of this case.",SRC001-s7,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1758:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_218_2016.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_218_2016.pdf,,SC,2016,Formal admissions recorded at the trial need no further proof unless the Court wants them to be proved.,"58. No fact need be proved in any proceeding which the parties thereto or their agents agree to admit at the hearing, or which, before the hearing, they agree to admit by any writing under their hands, or which by any rule of pleading in force at the time they are deemed to have admitted by their pleadings: Provided that the court may, in its discretion, require the facts admitted to be proved otherwise than by such admissions.",SRC029-s31,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1759:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_21_2017.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_21_2017.pdf,,SC,2017,A claim for the price of goods sold and delivered on an unwritten contract falls within section 8 of the Prescription Ordinance.,An action “for or in respect of any goods sold and delivered” will be prescribed by a period of one year even if it is based on an unwritten contract between the parties to the action in relation to such “goods sold and delivered”.,SRC001-s7,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1764:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_225_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_225_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,"A lessee or licensee cannot question the title of the person who gave them the lease or licence, and must first quit the land before challenging the title.","No tenant of immovable property, or person claiming through such tenant, shall during the continuance of the tenancy, be permitted to deny that the landlord of such tenant had, at the beginning of the tenancy, a title to such immovable property; and no person who came upon any immovable property by the licence of the person in possession thereof shall be permitted to deny that such person had a title to such possession at the time when such licence was given.",SRC029-s116,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1769:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_231_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_231_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,"A deed for the sale or transfer of land, being a document which is required by law to be attested, has to be proved in the manner set out in Section 68 of the Evidence Ordinance by proof that the maker (the vendor) of that document signed it in the presence of witnesses and notary.","A deed for the sale or transfer of land, being a document which is required by law to be attested, has to be proved in the manner set out in Section 68 of the Evidence Ordinance by proof that the maker (the vendor) of that document signed it in the presence of witnesses and notary.",SRC029-s68,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1771:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_232_2017.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_232_2017.pdf,,SC,2017,A licensee cannot claim prescriptive title against the owner or their heirs on the basis of the period they possessed the property.,"When a person starts possessing an immovable property with leave and licence of the owner, the presumption is that he continues to possess the immovable property on the permission originally granted and such a person or his agents or heirs cannot claim prescriptive title against the owner or his heirs on the basis of the period he possessed the property.",SRC029-s116,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1773:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_235_2016.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_235_2016.pdf,,SC,2016,"In order to succeed in a claim of prescriptive title, proof of undisturbed and uninterrupted possession, under a title adverse to or independent of that of the owner of the immovable property, for a period of ten years is required.","Proof of the undisturbed and uninterrupted possession by a defendant in any action, or by those under whom he claims, of lands or immovable property, by a title adverse to or independent of that of the claimant or plaintiff in such action (that is to say, a possession unaccompanied by payment of rent or produce, or performance of service or duty, or by any other act by the possessor, from which an acknowledgment of a right existing in another person would fairly and naturally be inferred) for ten years previous to the bringing of such action, shall entitle the defendant to a decree in his favour with costs.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1782:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_242_2016_amd1-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_242_2016_amd1-1.pdf,,SC,2016,A person cannot acquire prescriptive right to a property unless they produce an event or an overt act that manifests their intention to occupy the property as the owner.,the Respondent (Defendant) cannot acquire prescriptive right to the subject property unless he produces of an event or an overt act that manifest her intention to occupy the property as the owner as against the Petitioner (Appellant).,SRC071-s13,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1797:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_27_2023.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_27_2023.pdf,,SC,2023,"The standard of proof required for establishing a right of way under Section 69(2) of the Primary Courts' Procedure Act is 'actual use', not 'entitlement to use'.","It is sufficient, for the purpose of issuing such a declaration, that there is evidence of ‘actual use’ to substantiate one’s entitlement.",SRC001-s2,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1800:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_28_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_28_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,The burden of proving prescriptive right to a distinct and separate land is with the party claiming it.,once the paper title to the subject matter of this action is proved to be with the Plaintiff and the 1st Defendant the burden of proving the prescriptive right to the subject matter of this action as a distinct and separate land within the meaning of 5 SC/APPEAL/28/2014 section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance is with the 3rd Defendant which the 3rd Defendant has failed to do even on the finding of the Learned trial Judge as to the possession of the subject matter of this action?,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1801:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_28_2016.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_28_2016.pdf,,SC,2016,"The possession of a property by a defendant can be considered 'undisturbed and uninterrupted possession' if they have been in continuous possession for a period of time, as established by the Prescription Ordinance.","In the presence of the above evidence, it is not difficult to hold that the possession of this property by Defendant is ‘undisturbed and uninterrupted possession’ within the meaning of Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1803:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_29_2009.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_29_2009.pdf,,SC,2009,"A creditor who entered into a settlement in a Debt Conciliation Board in which a conditional transfer figured, is not prevented from later instituting a vindicatory action claiming title based on that conditional transfer.",I do not see that there can be any disability for the plaintiff to bring an action upon the title he had obtained by the deed of transfer in his favour upon the footing that there had been a default resulting in the right to redeem having come to an end.,SRC001-s43,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1805:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_29_2021.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_29_2021.pdf,,SC,2021,"A deed is considered duly attested if it is signed by the executant, the notary, and the witnesses at the same time, in the presence of one another.",An instrument which is required by section 2 of the Prevention of Frauds Ordinance to be notorially attested must be signed by the notary and the witnesses at the same time as the maker and in his presence.,SRC001-s2,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1811:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_30_2015.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_30_2015.pdf,,SC,2015,Gross ingratitude should be proved with certainty and with sound evidence.,If a party is able to prove gross ingratitude would deprive a person of a property right.,SRC001-s2,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1812:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_30_2016.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_30_2016.pdf,,SC,2016,A person asserting adverse possession has no equities in his favour. He alone must prove prescriptive title with cogent evidence.,A person asserting adverse possession has no equities in his favour. He alone must prove prescriptive title with cogent evidence.,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1821:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_32_2021.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_32_2021.pdf,,SC,2021,A party cannot take up new positions which are questions of fact for the first time on appeal.,The defendants cannot take up new positions which are questions of fact for the first time on appeal.,SRC029-s114,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1823:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_33_2009-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_33_2009-1.pdf,,SC,2009,The positive rule of law in Section 8 of the Prescription Ordinance applies when an action is filed after a lapse of one year after the date of the last invoice.,"In the circumstances, it is clear that the positive rule of law referred to in Section 8 of the Prescription Ordinance shall apply in this instance.",SRC071-s8,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1825:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_33_2010.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_33_2010.pdf,,SC,2010,A person cannot acquire prescriptive title to property without demonstrating an overt act showing an intention to possess adversely to the owner.,"Without any demonstration of any overt act against the rights of the owner, court cannot recognize Nandasekera as a person who has acted as one holding prescriptive possession against the owner who had given him leave and license to step in and live there, no matter how long he had enjoyed the property.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1828:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_33_2020.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_33_2020.pdf,,SC,2020,A defective power of attorney can be cured if the party ratifies the acts done by the lawyer on the defective proxy.,"If in fact he had his client’s authority to do so, then the defect is one which, in the absence of any positive legal bar, could be cured.",SRC014-s33,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1831:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_34_2015.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_34_2015.pdf,,SC,2015,A person who strikes another with a formidable weapon must be presumed to have known that the act is imminently dangerous to the life of the person at whom he strikes.,Thus a man who strikes at the back of another a violent blow with a formidable weapon must be taken to know that he is doing an act imminently dangerous to the life of the person at whom he strikes and that a probable result of his act will be to cause that person’s death.,SRC029-s6,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1836:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_36_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_36_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,"If no objection is made to a document being marked when it was first tendered in evidence, it becomes evidence for all the purposes of the case and it cannot be challenged only in appeal.","Hence, if no objection is made to a document being marked when it was first tendered in evidence, it becomes evidence for all the purposes of the case and it cannot be challenged only in appeal.",SRC029-s154,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1838:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_36_2019.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_36_2019.pdf,,SC,2019,The burden of proof rests fairly and squarely on the party invoking the provisions of Section 3 of the Prescription to establish a starting point for their acquisition of prescriptive rights.,"Where a party invokes the provisions of Section 3 of the Prescription, the burden of proof rests fairly and squarely on him to establish a starting point for his or her acquisition of prescriptive rights [Chelliah v. Wijenathan et al (54 N.L.R. 337)].",SRC001-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1844:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_38_2014-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_38_2014-1.pdf,,SC,2014,"A presumption arises that a Last Will has been destroyed by the Testator when the Last Will can be traced back to the Testator at the time of death, but cannot be found after the death.","Where the Last Will can be traced to the Testator and where the Last Will cannot be found with the Testator at the time of death, the presumption arises that the Last Will has been destroyed.",SRC001-s6,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1849:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_39_2024.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_39_2024.pdf,,SC,2024,A successful action for declaration of title interrupts prescription.,A successful action for declaration of title is an interruption of possession.,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1864:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_43_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_43_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,"The word 'owner' in Section 40 of the Forest Ordinance should be given a purposive interpretation, considering the intention of the legislature.",In my view the word “owner” as it occurs in Section 40 of the Forest Ordinance cannot be considered in isolation applying purely legal definition of the term “owner” but must be given a purposive interpretation taking into account the intention of the legislature.,SRC001-s40,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
