scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1876:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_45_2010.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_45_2010.pdf,,SC,2010,"A document required to be attested cannot be used as evidence unless its genuineness has been either admitted or established by proof, which should be given before the document is accepted by Court.","A document cannot be used in evidence, unless its genuineness has been either admitted or established by proof, which should be given before the document is accepted by Court.",SRC001-s68,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1878:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_45_2017.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_45_2017.pdf,,SC,2017,"A claim of prescription cannot lie in respect of an undivided share, but only in respect of a definite and identifiable portion of land.","In any event, it is well settled that a claim of prescription cannot lie in respect of an undivided share, but only in respect of a definite and identifiable portion of land.",SRC071-s3,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1886:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_48_2010.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_48_2010.pdf,,SC,2010,A notary cannot give evidence to support a disputed document without proper proof.,"will the named Notary (in P2 & P3) take it upon himself to give evidence and support the Defendant-Appellant-Petitioner’s case as far as V1 is concerned in breach of 11 the Prevention of Frauds Ordinance, Stamp Duty Ordinance, and the Notaries Ordinance?",SRC001-s11,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1888:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_48_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_48_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,A servitude may be acquired either by express grant or by prescription.,A servitude may be acquired either by express grant or by prescription.,SRC001-s2,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1898:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_4_2007.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_4_2007.pdf,,SC,2007,An acknowledgment of liability in writing can take a cause of action out of the limitation period imposed by the Prescription Ordinance.,"If so, in any event, is the cause of action of the Plaintiff-Appellants set out in the Plaint, taken out from the limitation imposed in terms of Section 8 of the Prescription Ordinance?",SRC071-s8,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
