scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1900:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_4_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_4_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,A contract may be avoided by Court on the ground of laesio enormis either when the purchaser pays more than double the true value of the thing or the vendor sells the thing for less than half its value.,A contract may be avoided by Court on the ground of laesio enormis either when the purchaser pays more than double the true value of the thing or the vendor sells the thing for less than half its value.,SRC001-s2,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1902:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_4_2016-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_4_2016-1.pdf,,SC,2016,"A party relying on a notarially executed deed must formally prove it by calling the necessary evidence, including attesting witnesses, as required by law.","Section 68; If a document is required by law to be attested, it should not be used as evidence until one attesting witness at least has been called for the purpose of proving its execution, if there be an attesting witness alive, and subject to the process of the court and capable of giving evidence",SRC014-s31,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1908:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_51_2010.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_51_2010.pdf,,SC,2010,A contract requires an offer and acceptance.,"every contract requires an offer and acceptance. Therefore an offer or promise which is not accepted, is not actionable [vide Justice Weerasooriya in Muthukuda v. Sumanatwathie, (1962) 65 NLR 205, 208, 209].",SRC029-s116,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1910:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_51_2020-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_51_2020-1.pdf,,SC,2020,Courts may countenance infirmities in proving original ownership of land if it is a realistic approach rather than a legalistic one.,"Courts by and large countenance infirmities in this regard, if infirmities they be, in an approach which is realistic rather than legalistic, as to do otherwise would be to put the relief given by partition decrees outside the reach of very many persons seeking to end the co-ownership.",SRC029-s32(5),none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1914:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_52_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_52_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,"An employer cannot transfer an employee from one company to another, whether it is an associate company or a subsidiary company, merely because the directors were the same.","The Court went onto observe, if the employer relying upon a term of contract which permit transfer of employees from one section or from one department to another, make use of such a course of action to move employees from one legal entity to another, it would offend the provisions of the Termination of Workmen Act.",SRC001-s2,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1916:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_53_2011.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_53_2011.pdf,,SC,2011,"A co-owner cannot acquire prescriptive title to undivided land, and a partition among co-owners terminates co-ownership only after undisturbed, uninterrupted, and exclusive possession of the divided lots for a period of over ten years.","Where a land is divided with the consent of all the co-owners but no cross conveyances are executed in respect of the lots, co-ownership terminates only after undisturbed, uninterrupted and exclusive possession of the divided lots for a period of over ten years",SRC071-s3,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1919:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_53_2019.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_53_2019.pdf,,SC,2019,A party invoking the provisions of section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance must establish a starting point for their acquisition of prescriptive rights with consistent and credible evidence.,"In order to defeat the ownership of an adverse claimant to immovable property, the burden of proof rests squarely and fairly on him to establish a starting point for his or her acquisition of prescriptive rights (Chelliah vs. Wijenathan 54 NLR 337, Solomon Dias vs. William Singho and others [2015] 1 SLR 277).",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1925:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_54_2020.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_54_2020.pdf,,SC,2020,A defence of prescription must be specially pleaded by way of defence and cannot be raised for the first time in appeal.,"Basnayaka CJ, in Brampy Appuhamy v Gunasekera (supra) has held that (at p. 255); “[I]t is settled law that when, as in the case of Sections 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10 and 11 of the Prescription Ordinance, the effect of the statute is merely to limit the time in which an action may be brought and not to extinguish the right, the Court will not take the statute into account unless it is specially pleaded by way of defence”.",SRC071-s5,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1927:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_54a_2008.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_54a_2008.pdf,,SC,2008,"A plaintiff must adduce adequate and reliable evidence to establish prescriptive title, including assertive evidence of adverse possession as against mere evidence of occupation.","However, the evidence of the defendant and her son does not suggest that they did any such thing. In this connection, the defendant does not claim that she made any statement to the plaintiff‟s father that she does not accept his title. The defendant does not claim that, after the plaintiff‟s father obtained title, she made any alterations to the property or paid the rates and taxes in respect of the property in her name or obtained electricity and water connections in her name. The defendant has not led the evidence of the Grama Sevaka or her neighbours to suggest that she was considered to be holding the property in her own right after the property was transferred to the plaintiff‟s father in 1978.",SRC071-s13,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1929:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_55_2015.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_55_2015.pdf,,SC,2015,"A judge must consider all prayers in a plaint, even in an ex parte trial.","Even in an ex parte trial, the judge must act according to law and ensure that the relief claimed is due in fact and in law, and must dismiss the plaintiff's claim if he is not entitled to it. An ex parte judgment cannot be entered without a hearing and an adjudication.",SRC029-s68,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1931:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_55_2020.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_55_2020.pdf,,SC,2020,A party must prove the due execution of a document to establish its validity.,"The learned judge has noted that both the 2nd Defendant and the Plaintiff have not discharged the burden cast on them by section 68 of the Evidence Ordinance read with section 2 of the Prevention of Frauds Ordinance, which required them to prove that the two documents",SRC029-s68,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1934:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_56_2023.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_56_2023.pdf,,SC,2023,A plea of prescription requires ten years of uninterrupted and undisturbed possession.,"Proof of the undisturbed and uninterrupted possession by a defendant in any action, or by those under whom he claims, of lands or immovable property, by a title adverse to or independent of that of the claimant or plaintiff in such action (that is to say, a possession unaccompanied by payment of rent or produce, or performance of service or duty, or by any other act by the possessor, from which an acknowledgment of a right existing in another person would fairly and naturally be inferred) for ten years previous to the bringing of such action, shall entitle the defendant to a decree in his favour with costs...",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1940:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_58_2019.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_58_2019.pdf,,SC,2019,A person who possesses a land with secret intention cannot claim that his possession is an adverse possession.,A person who possesses a land with secret intention cannot claim that his possession is an adverse possession. Possession of a land by a person with secret intention cannot be considered as an adverse possession in terms of Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance.,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1946:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_59_2021.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_59_2021.pdf,,SC,2021,A court must consider the relevance of evidence before admitting it.,"Evidence may be given in any suit or proceeding of the existence or non-existence of every fact in issue, and of such other facts as are hereinafter declared to be relevant and of no others.",SRC029-s5,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1948:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_5_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_5_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,"A party claiming prescription must prove the commencement of adverse possession, which includes an act of ouster, prior to the running of prescription.",8 unless a party is able to prove that there had been an act of ouster prior to the running of prescription.,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1950:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_60_2004.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_60_2004.pdf,,SC,2004,The failure to challenge evidence by cross-examination may not be sufficient to hold that a particular fact had been proved within the meaning of Section 3 of the Evidence Ordinance.,"It might, however, be a factor to be taken into account in accepting such evidence.",SRC029-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
