scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:2059:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_95_2018.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_95_2018.pdf,,SC,2018,Uncontradicted evidence adduced by a party is a fact in favour of that party's case.,"In other words, uncontradicted evidence adduced by the 2nd Defendant in the trial is a fact in favour of the 2nd Defendant’s case.",SRC029-s03,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:2062:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_96_2010.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_96_2010.pdf,,SC,2010,"A period of prescription can be 'tacked' onto another period, and the progress of prescription is not arrested by a disability such as minority or absence beyond seas.","As far as this case is concerned, the period from 1954 to 1962 [7 years] can be ‘tacked’ onto the period from 1979 to 1984 [6 years] in computing the period the Defendants were in possession for the purpose of term of prescription and regarding the application of the proviso to Section 3 of the Ordinance the progress of prescription is not arrested subject, however, to discounting the period Rosa enjoyed life interest to the corpus.",SRC071-s13,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:2068:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_97_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_97_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,A possessory action presupposes the existence of possession ut dominus and a dispossession otherwise than by process of law.,A possessory action presupposes the existence of possession ut dominus and a dispossession otherwise than by process of law.,SRC071-s4,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:2077:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_9_2016.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_9_2016.pdf,,SC,2016,A notary who attests a document in terms of Prevention of Frauds Ordinance is generally competent to testify under section 68 of the Evidence Ordinance.,A notary who attests a deed is an attesting witness within the meaning of that expression in section 68 and section 69 of the Evidence Ordinance.,SRC001-s68,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:2078:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_9_2017.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_9_2017.pdf,,SC,2017,"Once paper title becomes undisputed, the burden shifts to the defendants to show that they have independent rights in the form of prescription as claimed by them.",Once paper title became undisputed the burden shifted to the defendants to show that they had independent rights in the form of prescription as claimed by them.,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:2086:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_14_2016.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_14_2016.pdf,,SC,2016,Silence in response to letters of demand amounts to admission of liability.,"In other words, there was a failure to respond to the letters of demand. These letters of demand were also marked in evidence and they had not been challenged. In the circumstances, it would appear that the Defendants admitted the liability to pay the monies mentioned in the said letters of demand.",SRC014-s31,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:2087:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_16_2007.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_16_2007.pdf,,SC,2007,The President's belief in the necessity or expediency of an emergency regulation is deemed conclusive of its validity.,"As established in the case of Yasapala, the President's belief in the necessity or expediency of an emergency regulation is deemed conclusive of its validity, as he is not bound as a matter of law to disclose the reasons for the proclamation.",SRC001-s2,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:2088:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_19_2013-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_19_2013-1.pdf,,SC,2013,A party may lead evidence aliunde to controvert the consideration stated in a deed.,"By reason of proviso (1) to Section 92 of the Evidence Ordinance, a party is not per se precluded from leading evidence aliunde to controvert the consideration stated in a deed.",SRC029-s92,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:2089:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_1_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_1_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,A party cannot recover a debt if the amount due has prescribed under the Prescription Ordinance.,"Focusing his attention on the sums of money purportedly due to the Plaintiff from the Defendant on account of the ‘running account’ (“P5” to “P23”), the learned Judge has noted that the amount due per each invoice had become prescribed (as per section 8 of the Prescription Ordinance) as a result of action not having been filed within 1 year of the last payment due.",SRC071-s8,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
