scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1077:draftly-rule-courts-coa-c.a-dc-326_1997.pdf-1,courts-coa-c.a-dc-326_1997.pdf,,COA,1997,The formalities required to be observed when transferring and/or selling a land or other immovable property as laid down in Section 2 of the Prevention of Frauds Ordinance were not followed when executing the deed.,"In terms of Section 2 of the Prevention of Frauds Ordinance a sale or transfer of land has to be in writing signed by two or more witnesses before a Notary, duly attested by the Notary and the witnesses. If this is not done, the document and its contents cannot be used in evidence [Samarakaan v. Gunasekera and Another (2011) 1 SrLL.R. 149].",SRC001-s2,ok,Section 2 of the Prevention of Frauds Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1084:draftly-rule-courts-coa-c.a.-dc_1043_1997.pdf-1,courts-coa-c.a.-dc_1043_1997.pdf,,COA,1997,"The burden of seeking and getting evidence before court, in the course of investigation of title to the land sought to be partitioned by parties before Court, prior to deciding what share should go to which party is more the duty of the judge than the contesting parties.","The burden of seeking and getting evidence before court, in the course of investigation of title to the land sought to be partitioned by parties before Court, prior to deciding what share should go to which party is more the duty of the judge than the contesting parties.",SRC029-s110,section-not-found,section 110 of the Evidence Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1095:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca-423-2000-f-22.1.2019.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca-423-2000-f-22.1.2019.pdf,,COA,2019,A person who claims prescriptive title against the rightful owner who has the paper title has a very heavy burden to prove all the requirements prescribed in section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance.,A person who claims prescriptive title against the rightful owner who has the paper title has a very heavy burden to prove all the requirements prescribed in section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance.,SRC071-s3,none,section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1096:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca-472-97-16.01.19.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca-472-97-16.01.19.pdf,,COA,,"A party claiming prescriptive title must prove undisturbed and uninterrupted possession, independent or adverse to the claimant, for ten years prior to the action.","In order to initiate a prescriptive title, it is necessary to show a change in the nature of the possession and the party claiming prescriptive right should show an ouster.",SRC029-s3,section-not-found,Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1105:draftly-rule-courts-coa-CA-PHC-126-20-Judgment-Final.pdf-1,courts-coa-CA-PHC-126-20-Judgment-Final.pdf,,COA,,The pendency of civil proceedings does not constitute a legal bar to the institution or continuation of proceedings under Section 66.,"At its highest, the existence of such proceedings may be considered as surrounding circumstance, however, it does not undermine the Magistrate’s duty to inquire into the existence of a dispute affecting land and the likelihood of a breach of the peace, and to make such provisional orders as are necessary in that regard.",SRC001-s66,section-not-found,Section 66 of the Primary Courts’ Procedure Act,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1108:draftly-rule-courts-coa-CA-PHC-93-24-Judgment-Final.pdf-1,courts-coa-CA-PHC-93-24-Judgment-Final.pdf,,COA,,"A power of attorney holder does not have locus standi to be named as a party and appear in revision proceedings under the Maintenance Act No. 37 of 1999, unless the power of attorney explicitly grants such authority.","It is a fundamental principle of the law relating to power of attorneys that the authority conferred upon an attorney must be strictly construed, and that an attorney cannot act beyond the scope of the authority expressly granted.",SRC001-s8,section-not-found,"Section 8 of the Maintenance Act, No. 37 of 1999",True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1121:draftly-rule-courts-coa-CA-WRT-0385-23-1.pdf-1,courts-coa-CA-WRT-0385-23-1.pdf,,COA,,"The Board must consider all circumstances of the case, including the language of the notarial instrument of transfer, any difference between the sum received by the transferor and the value of the property transferred, the continuance of the transferor's possession of the property transferred, and the existence of any agreement between the transferor and the transferee, when deciding whether or not a transfer or conditional transfer is in reality a mortgage.","In any Proceedings under the Ordinance in regard to an application relating to a transfer or conditional transfer of immovable property, the Bond shall, notwithstanding anything to the contrary in section 2 of the Prevention of Frauds Ordinance, or sections 91 and 92 of the Evidence Ordinance and for the purpose of deciding whether or not such transfer or conditional transfer is in reality a mortgage, take into consideration all the circumstances of the case and in particular the following matters (a) The language of the notarial instrument of transfer and where provision in regard to the right of the transferor or any other person to redeem or purchase the property transferred is contained in any other notarial instrument.",SRC001-s2,ok,section 2 of the Prevention of Frauds Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1134:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_242_99.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_242_99.pdf,,COA,,"A party invoking the provisions of section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance to defeat the ownership of an adverse claimant to immovable property must establish a starting point for their acquisition of prescriptive rights, which must be undisturbed, uninterrupted, adverse to or independent of that of the former possessor and must have lasted for at least ten years.","According to section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance such a possession must be undisturbed, uninterrupted, adverse to or independent of that of the former possessor and should have lasted for at least ten years before he could transform such possession into prescriptive title.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
